    "notes": "I implemented both functions in `src/solution.py`, and every test passes: 74 of 74 in the public suite and 212 of 212 in the private suite.\n\n- **`parse_duration`** trims whitespace from both ends of the text, then reads it one component at a time. It rejects:\n  - anything that isn't a string;\n  - empty text, or a number with no unit after it;\n  - leading zeros, and digits that aren't ASCII 0–9 (such as `３`);\n  - unknown or uppercase units, and anything between components (spaces, commas);\n  - units that are out of order or repeated;\n  - totals above 2**53 − 1.\n- **Long counts:** a count with more than 16 digits is rejected as too large before it's converted. Without that check, Python's own error for very long digit strings would come through, and its message doesn't contain \"duration\".\n- **`format_duration`** rejects bools and anything that isn't an int with `TypeError`, and values outside 0 to 2**53 − 1 with `ValueError`. It writes the largest units first, leaves out zero components, and returns `\"0ms\"` for zero.\n\nTests left failing: none",
